# Good management of EOL and encoding in GIT is painful if no well managed
#
# This file allows the following.
#  - Enforce line endings in the whole repository, it is based on behavior of new GIT versions which default to LF line 
#    endings when 'text' is specified.
#  - Enforce good encoding for text files, if a text file is not encoded in UTF-8 then GIT will automatically change the
#    encoding to UTF-8.
#
# To get log more details about the configuration provided in this file you can take time to read the following pages.
#  - https://adaptivepatchwork.com/2012/03/01/mind-the-end-of-your-line/
#  - https://stackoverflow.com/questions/170961/whats-the-strategy-for-handling-crlf-carriage-return-line-feed-with-git#answer-10855862
#  - https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/getting-started-with-git/configuring-git-to-handle-line-endings#refreshing-a-repository-after-changing-line-endings
#  - https://git-scm.com/docs/gitattributes
#  - https://github.com/alexkaratarakis/gitattributes
#  - https://buzut.net/cours/versioning-avec-git/normalisation-des-fichiers
#  - https://stackoverflow.com/questions/59434963/existing-git-repository-with-files-in-mixed-encodings
#
# IMPORTANT: If this is the first time you configure this file in your project you should refresh your repository to 
#            rewrite all the files with the new LF line endings and UTF-8 encoding. Follow the below link for detailed
#            instructions.
#
#            https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/getting-started-with-git/configuring-git-to-handle-line-endings#refreshing-a-repository-after-changing-line-endings
#
#            After execution of the 'git add --renormalize .' command you could still have files with CRLF line endings
#            or bad encoding locally. Push your changes on a new branch and then checkout it again in an other folder to
#            check it again.
#
#            To ensure that all your files have LF line endings enter the following command.
#
#                git ls-files --stage --abbrev --eol
#
#            It should display 'i/lf' on the fourth column and 'w/lf' on the fifth one. If this is not the case for all 
#            files you can terminate the conversions on specific files with the 'dos2unix' command.
#
#            If you would like to manually change the encoding of a file you can use the 'iconv' command.
#
#                iconv options -f from-encoding -t to-encoding inputfile inputfile(s) -o outputfile outputfile(s)
#
#            Finally, to check both the line endings and the encoding of a specific file you can use the 'file' command.
#
#                file my-file.txt
#                file -i my-file.txt
#

# Line endings and file encoding configuration
#
# This section instructs GIT to do the following.
#  - Detect the file types (i.e. text or binary) and convert the line endings to LF when the type is text.
#  - Convert text files which are not encoded in UTF-8 to UTF-8.
#
# The following practices should be followed to edit this list.
#  - The 'diff' attribute can only be specified if the file type is present in the "builtin patterns" documented at 
#    https://git-scm.com/docs/gitattributes
#  - All text files (generally source code and documentation) should be configured with the 'text' attribute.
#  - All binary files (generally images, videos, and executables) should be configured with the 'binary' attribute.
#  - To easier reading this list should be alphabetically sorted.
.gitattributes  text working-tree-encoding=UTF-8
.gitignore      text working-tree-encoding=UTF-8
.tool-versions  text working-tree-encoding=UTF-8
Dockerfile      text working-tree-encoding=UTF-8
Makefile        text working-tree-encoding=UTF-8
Vagrantfile     text working-tree-encoding=UTF-8
